  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how lymph is taken up from tissues and transported through the lymphatic system. On the left, blind-ended lymphatic capillaries sit within the extracellular matrix and receive interstitial fluid. Their overlapping endothelial edges are held by anchoring filaments and “button” junctions, which appear to act like flap-like openings that let fluid enter from the surrounding tissue.\n\nMoving right, the capillary transitions into larger collecting lymphatic vessels. Here the vessel wall becomes more continuous, with “zipper” junctions rather than the more discontinuous button-like contacts. The collectors are wrapped by lymphatic muscle cells, suggesting active contraction to propel lymph forward. Internal lymphatic valves divide the vessel into segments and enforce one-way flow, preventing backflow as lymph is pushed onward.\n\nOverall, the image shows a functional progression from passive uptake in delicate initial capillaries to active, directed transport in muscular collecting vessels. The flow path is summarized by arrows leading from peripheral tissue into lymphatic capillaries, then through collector vessels, onward to a lymph node, and finally toward the thoracic duct.",
